1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,614
This guide shows you how glove tips help you hold speed through the corner, and how a cut resistant glove protects your hand when things go wrong.

2
00:00:09,614 --> 00:00:16,463
You will learn how to choose tips, where to fit them, how to size your gloves, and when to replace them.

3
00:00:16,463 --> 00:00:21,337
Gloves get the least attention of any short track kit, and the most abuse.

4
00:00:21,337 --> 00:00:23,773
Skaters obsess over blades and boots.

5
00:00:23,773 --> 00:00:31,808
Then they drag one hand along the ice at forty kilometres per hour, lap after lap, on a glove they bought two seasons ago.

6
00:00:31,808 --> 00:00:33,652
That hand is not decoration.

7
00:00:33,652 --> 00:00:40,171
It is a reference point and a balance aid, and on a tight radius it is part of how you hold a line.

8
00:00:40,871 --> 00:00:44,223
Many new skaters think the hand down is for support.

9
00:00:44,223 --> 00:00:45,319
It should not be.

10
00:00:45,319 --> 00:00:47,318
You are not leaning on the ice.

11
00:00:47,318 --> 00:00:50,734
On a well ridden corner, your hand is a light sensor.

12
00:00:50,734 --> 00:00:52,668
It tells you where the ice is.

13
00:00:52,668 --> 00:00:54,473
It confirms your lean angle.

14
00:00:54,473 --> 00:01:01,370
And it gives your brain a fixed point of reference while your body does something quite unnatural at speed.

15
00:01:01,370 --> 00:01:04,400
So what you want from that hand is consistency.

16
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:08,912
It should slide the same way every lap and send you the same feedback.

17
00:01:08,912 --> 00:01:11,426
It should never suddenly grab or catch.

18
00:01:11,426 --> 00:01:15,938
A tip that grips when you do not expect it does more than scrub speed.

19
00:01:15,938 --> 00:01:20,450
It can pull your shoulder, break your line, and put you into the pads.

20
00:01:21,150 --> 00:01:24,002
Here is a skater leaning through a corner.

21
00:01:24,002 --> 00:01:27,873
The blue line is the body, tilted away from the vertical.

22
00:01:27,873 --> 00:01:30,318
The orange arc marks the lean angle.

23
00:01:30,318 --> 00:01:34,461
The green dot is the inside hand, resting lightly on the ice.

24
00:01:34,461 --> 00:01:40,437
Along the bottom, the orange arrow shows drag on the hand, and that drag slows you down.

25
00:01:40,437 --> 00:01:44,104
The panel on the right lists what a glove tip changes.

26
00:01:44,104 --> 00:01:46,549
You get a smooth, hard contact face.

27
00:01:46,549 --> 00:01:49,809
You get the same feel on lap one and on lap ten.

28
00:01:49,809 --> 00:01:52,253
And you get no sudden grab or catch.

29
00:01:52,253 --> 00:01:54,630
Bare fabric gives you none of this.

30
00:01:55,330 --> 00:01:57,850
Bare fabric on ice is unpredictable.

31
00:01:57,850 --> 00:01:58,690
It gets wet.

32
00:01:58,690 --> 00:01:59,460
It freezes.

33
00:01:59,460 --> 00:02:01,910
It wears through in a few sessions.

34
00:02:01,910 --> 00:02:09,891
And the amount of drag changes from your first lap to your last, as the fabric picks up moisture and ice crystals.

35
00:02:09,891 --> 00:02:11,291
Glove tips fix this.

36
00:02:11,291 --> 00:02:16,821
They put a hard, smooth, low friction surface between your fingers and the ice.

37
00:02:16,821 --> 00:02:18,991
That is the whole job of a tip.

38
00:02:18,991 --> 00:02:19,691
Less drag.

39
00:02:19,691 --> 00:02:21,091
More predictability.

40
00:02:21,091 --> 00:02:28,231
More speed carried through the apex, instead of bleeding it away every time your fingers make contact.

41
00:02:28,231 --> 00:02:31,801
You can skate without tips, and beginners often do.

42
00:02:31,801 --> 00:02:37,052
Most skaters fit them as soon as they are putting a hand down consistently.

43
00:02:37,752 --> 00:02:41,411
The guide compares two families of StayBent Quick Tips.

44
00:02:41,411 --> 00:02:45,802
The solid colour version is the sensible default for most skaters.

45
00:02:45,802 --> 00:02:49,794
It comes in five colours: red, green, blue, black and white.

46
00:02:49,794 --> 00:02:54,585
It does the core job without complication, and it is the cheaper way in.

47
00:02:54,585 --> 00:02:58,843
That matters while you are still learning where your hand lands.

48
00:02:58,843 --> 00:03:03,767
The carbon fibre version uses a carbon weave set in strong, durable resin.

49
00:03:03,767 --> 00:03:09,489
Carbon is stiff for its weight, so the tip holds its shape and its surface under load.

50
00:03:09,489 --> 00:03:12,017
Which one suits you depends on volume.

51
00:03:12,017 --> 00:03:20,267
If you train corners several times a week, or you are hard on kit, carbon earns its place because you replace it less often.

52
00:03:20,267 --> 00:03:26,122
If you skate twice a week and your tips last a season, solid colour is the better value.

53
00:03:26,822 --> 00:03:30,364
How many tips you need is personal, and worth testing.

54
00:03:30,364 --> 00:03:38,168
Many skaters only fit tips to the fingers that actually make contact, usually the ones on the outside edge of the hand.

55
00:03:38,168 --> 00:03:39,742
Look at your own gloves.

56
00:03:39,742 --> 00:03:46,170
The wear pattern tells you the truth about your hand position better than any coach at the boards.

57
00:03:46,170 --> 00:03:50,367
If only two fingers are scuffed, that is where your tips belong.

58
00:03:50,367 --> 00:03:54,237
If nothing is scuffed at all, that tells you something too.

59
00:03:54,237 --> 00:04:01,648
Either you are not putting a hand down consistently yet, or you press hard on one or two laps and float the rest.

60
00:04:01,648 --> 00:04:03,222
Fix the technique first.

61
00:04:03,222 --> 00:04:05,058
Then buy tips to support it.

62
00:04:05,058 --> 00:04:08,534
And on a standard oval, the inside hand is your left.

63
00:04:08,534 --> 00:04:09,912
Fit that glove first.

64
00:04:10,612 --> 00:04:12,160
Tips handle the corner.

65
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:14,785
Cut resistant fabric handles the crash.

66
00:04:14,785 --> 00:04:20,574
Short track puts several skaters on a small radius with sharpened steel on their feet.

67
00:04:20,574 --> 00:04:24,882
Your hands are low, forward, and close to other people's blades.

68
00:04:24,882 --> 00:04:27,641
That is simply the geometry of the sport.

69
00:04:27,641 --> 00:04:31,815
A cut resistant glove adds a protective layer for that moment.

70
00:04:31,815 --> 00:04:33,767
But be clear about one thing.

71
00:04:33,767 --> 00:04:36,325
Cut resistant does not mean cut proof.

72
00:04:36,325 --> 00:04:44,065
These fabrics are designed to resist a blade and slow it, so a very serious cut becomes something far less serious.

73
00:04:44,065 --> 00:04:46,893
No glove makes a sharpened blade harmless.

74
00:04:46,893 --> 00:04:52,143
So wear the protection, and also skate aware of where other people's feet are.

75
00:04:52,143 --> 00:04:53,085
You need both.

76
00:04:53,085 --> 00:04:58,605
Protective rules vary by event, so check the rules for each competition you enter.

77
00:04:59,305 --> 00:05:01,691
This diagram shows a glove from above.

78
00:05:01,691 --> 00:05:06,213
The two fingers marked in green are the ones that usually touch the ice.

79
00:05:06,213 --> 00:05:10,169
Your own wear pattern decides this, not the shape of the glove.

80
00:05:10,169 --> 00:05:13,937
On the right are four fit checks to run before every season.

81
00:05:13,937 --> 00:05:17,768
The glove should be snug across the palm, knuckle to knuckle.

82
00:05:17,768 --> 00:05:21,159
Your fingertips should reach the end without bunching.

83
00:05:21,159 --> 00:05:25,241
There should be no loose fabric to pinch on the back of the hand.

84
00:05:25,241 --> 00:05:28,381
And the glove should not rotate when you twist it.

85
00:05:28,381 --> 00:05:32,275
A loose glove moves your tips away from where you fitted them.

86
00:05:32,275 --> 00:05:40,187
If you fall between sizes, take the smaller one if the glove stretches, or the larger one if you wear a thin liner underneath.

87
00:05:40,888 --> 00:05:46,543
Fitting is where most people rush, then wonder why their tips fall off mid session.

88
00:05:46,543 --> 00:05:48,247
Take ten careful minutes.

89
00:05:48,247 --> 00:05:49,678
First, clean and dry.

90
00:05:49,678 --> 00:05:53,017
Adhesive and epoxy both hate moisture and grease.

91
00:05:53,017 --> 00:05:55,947
Wipe the fingertips and let them dry fully.

92
00:05:55,947 --> 00:05:58,059
Second, mark before you commit.

93
00:05:58,059 --> 00:06:04,124
Put the glove on, get into your hand down position, and see where contact really happens.

94
00:06:04,124 --> 00:06:08,008
Fit the tips there, not where the glove pattern suggests.

95
00:06:08,008 --> 00:06:09,438
Third, cure properly.

96
00:06:09,438 --> 00:06:15,639
Give the adhesive the full cure time the maker specifies, at room temperature, undisturbed.

97
00:06:15,639 --> 00:06:18,774
Never fit tips the night before a competition.

98
00:06:18,774 --> 00:06:21,295
Do it a week out, then train in them.

99
00:06:21,295 --> 00:06:26,065
If tips keep coming off, it is almost always one of these three steps.

100
00:06:26,765 --> 00:06:28,653
Gloves come off the ice wet.

101
00:06:28,653 --> 00:06:33,913
Left in a bag, they smell, the fabric degrades, and the adhesive bond suffers.

102
00:06:33,913 --> 00:06:38,634
Take them out, open them up, and let them air dry at room temperature.

103
00:06:38,634 --> 00:06:44,366
Keep them away from heaters, because heat softens adhesive and warps composite parts.

104
00:06:44,366 --> 00:06:48,952
In a humid Indian summer, a fan does more good than any heat source.

105
00:06:48,952 --> 00:06:52,122
Every few sessions, run a finger over each tip.

106
00:06:52,122 --> 00:06:57,584
Check for a rough or scored surface, a worn profile, and any lifting at the edge.

107
00:06:57,584 --> 00:07:00,821
A lifting tip will come off at the worst moment.

108
00:07:00,821 --> 00:07:04,261
On the glove, look for thinning or an opened weave.

109
00:07:04,261 --> 00:07:06,014
There is no set lap count.

110
00:07:06,014 --> 00:07:08,712
Replace on condition, never on calendar.

111
00:07:08,712 --> 00:07:12,893
And after any big blade change, check your wear pattern again.

112
00:07:13,593 --> 00:07:15,432
Here is the short version.

113
00:07:15,432 --> 00:07:18,190
Your hands do real work in short track.

114
00:07:18,190 --> 00:07:23,069
Give them low friction tips so the corner behaves the way you expect.

115
00:07:23,069 --> 00:07:27,878
Put cut resistant fabric between your skin and other people's steel.

116
00:07:27,878 --> 00:07:29,363
Size everything snug.

117
00:07:29,363 --> 00:07:31,273
Dry it after every session.

118
00:07:31,273 --> 00:07:34,172
And replace it on condition, not on hope.

119
00:07:34,172 --> 00:07:41,245
The full guide is on the LiveLica blog, with more detail on choosing tips, sizing, fitting and care.

120
00:07:41,245 --> 00:07:43,437
The link is in the description.
